From: Rasheed <99349713+gitRasheed@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:47:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 31/61] NNS.stack: code character responses and predictors in
 sorted level order

A character response was coded by first appearance (unique(DV.train)),
while a factor response uses levels() and NNS.boost uses factor()
(sorted). Because NNS treats the codes as ordinal in a regression, the
result depended on row order and differed from the factor path. Character
predictors had the same first-appearance ordering (dummy column order).
Use sorted levels for both, as factor() does.

Before -> after (iris shuffled so virginica comes first, CLASS, default
5-fold CV; work/STK/ab/cases/iris_shuf):
  factor(y):        levels setosa/versicolor/virginica, OOF acc 0.985 (unchanged)
  as.character(y):  levels virginica/setosa/versicolor, OOF acc 0.815,
                    test labels include setosa for virginica rows
                 -> levels setosa/versicolor/virginica, OOF acc 0.985,
                    test labels identical to the factor path
Character predictor g in {zeta, alpha, mid}: dim.red coefficient order
  x g__zeta g__alpha g__mid -> x g__alpha g__mid g__zeta (= factor path)
Factor and numeric inputs are unchanged.
---
 R/Stack.R | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/R/Stack.R b/R/Stack.R
index 692df36..f371b29 100644
--- a/R/Stack.R
+++ b/R/Stack.R
@@ -209,7 +209,9 @@ NNS.stack <- function(IVs.train,
       } else if (is.factor(tr) || is.character(tr)) {
         tr_chr <- as.character(tr)
         te_chr <- as.character(te)
-        lev <- if (is.factor(tr)) levels(tr) else unique(tr_chr)
+        # Character predictors use sorted levels, as factor() and NNS.boost do,
+        # so the encoding does not depend on row order.
+        lev <- if (is.factor(tr)) levels(tr) else sort(unique(tr_chr))
         unseen <- setdiff(unique(te_chr), lev)
         if (length(unseen)) {
           stop(sprintf(
@@ -403,7 +405,9 @@ NNS.stack <- function(IVs.train,
       class_values <- levels(response_factor)
       y <- as.integer(response_factor)
     } else if (response_was_character) {
-      class_values <- unique(DV.train)
+      # Sorted (factor) level order, as for a factor response and in
+      # NNS.boost; first-appearance coding made results depend on row order.
+      class_values <- levels(factor(DV.train))
       y <- match(DV.train, class_values)
     } else if (response_was_logical) {
       class_values <- sort(unique(DV.train))
